.ie \n(.g .ds Aq \(aq
.el .ds Aq '
.TH mention 1  "mention " 
.SH NAME
mention \- Add, remove, or list reference edges (free\-text short\-id citations), in either direction
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBmention\fR [\fB\-h\fR|\fB\-\-help\fR] <\fIsubcommands\fR>
.SH DESCRIPTION
Add, remove, or list reference edges (free\-text short\-id citations), in either direction
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Print help
.SH SUBCOMMANDS
.TP
mention\-add(1)
Record that `from` cites the short\-id `to` in its free text (no blocking)
.TP
mention\-remove(1)
Remove the `from \-> to` reference
.TP
mention\-list(1)
List the short\-ids `id` mentions
.TP
mention\-by(1)
List the items that mention `id` — the reverse of `list` — in id order, closed and archived mentioners included. `\-\-type`, `\-\-in` and `\-\-label` narrow it as they narrow `next`; `\-\-limit` shows only the head and says so; `\-\-paginate` adds a token for the next page. Under `\-\-json` the records are `list \-\-json`\*(Aqs, as a plain array — or, with `\-\-limit`, as `{"items": [...], "total": <n>}`, plus `"next_token"` (a string, or null on the last page) and `"restarted"` (bool) with `\-\-paginate`, exactly as `next \-\-limit \-\-json` wraps them
.TP
mention\-help(1)
Print this message or the help of the given subcommand(s)
